            {
              "id": "glog-1b6f33ce-b777-45d1-8905-74c4a1f9c418",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `malloc` function in C++ is used to dynamically allocate memory at runtime. However, it can be a source of vulnerabilities if not used properly. If the `max_alloc_size` is user-controlled or can be manipulated to be an extremely large value, it can lead to a Denial of Service (DoS) attack by exhausting the system's memory. This is known as an Integer Overflow or Wraparound vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user input. Never trust user input blindly.\n2. Implement checks to ensure that the size argument passed to `malloc` is not excessively large.\n3. Use functions that limit the amount of memory that can be allocated, such as `malloc_s`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a simple fix for the vulnerability:\n\n```cpp\n#include <stdlib.h>\n\nif (max_alloc_size > MAX_ALLOWED_SIZE) {\n    // Handle error\n} else {\n    void* ptr = malloc(max_alloc_size);\n    if (ptr == NULL) {\n        // Handle error\n    }\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, `MAX_ALLOWED_SIZE` is a predefined constant that represents the maximum amount of memory that can be safely allocated.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `stdlib.h` library to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP Secure Coding Practices](https://owasp.org/www-project-secure-coding-practices-quick-reference-guide/)\n- [CWE-190: Integer Overflow or Wraparound](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/190.html)"
              },
