            {
              "id": "glog-98453598-928c-4bfa-b733-90ac6556103c",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strlen()` function in C++ is used to find the length of a string. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function counts the number of characters in a string until it reaches a null character (`\\0`). If the string is not null-terminated, `strlen()` will continue reading memory until it encounters a null character, which can lead to buffer overflows, memory corruption, and other unexpected behavior.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, `strlen(gpu_result.gpu_result.msg)`, there is a potential vulnerability if `gpu_result.gpu_result.msg` is not null-terminated. This could lead to a buffer overflow vulnerability, which can be exploited to execute arbitrary code, crash the program, or cause other security issues.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, ensure that all strings are null-terminated before passing them to `strlen()`. Additionally, consider using safer alternatives to `strlen()`, such as `strnlen()`, which takes a maximum length parameter and will not read past this length, even if it does not encounter a null character.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the provided code snippet:\n\n```cpp\n// Ensure gpu_result.gpu_result.msg is null-terminated\ngpu_result.gpu_result.msg[sizeof(gpu_result.gpu_result.msg) - 1] = '\\0';\n\n// Use strnlen() instead of strlen()\nsize_t length = strnlen(gpu_result.gpu_result.msg, sizeof(gpu_result.gpu_result.msg));\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe `strlen()` function is part of the C standard library (`<cstring>` in C++), so this library must be included for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-119: Improper Restriction of Operations within the Bounds of a Memory Buffer](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/119.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
