            {
              "id": "glog-8c175b13-e750-4d68-a2fa-bf8ac85883d8",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe `strncpy` function in C++ is used to copy a string from source to destination. However, it can lead to vulnerabilities if not used properly. The function does not automatically append a null character at the end of the destination string. If the source string is longer than the destination buffer, the destination string will not be null-terminated, which can lead to buffer overflow vulnerabilities.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use safer string handling functions that automatically handle null termination and buffer sizes, such as `strlcpy` or `strncpy_s`. If these are not available, you should manually ensure that the destination string is null-terminated.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the code:\n\n```cpp\n#include <cstring>\n\n// ...\n\nchar dest[MAX_SIZE];\nconst char* src = \"source string\";\nsize_t n = sizeof(dest);\n\nstrncpy(dest, src, n - 1);\ndest[n - 1] = '\\0';  // Ensure null termination\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<cstring>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP C++ Secure Coding Practices](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/C-Based_Toolchain_Hardening_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-120: Buffer Copy without Checking Size of Input ('Classic Buffer Overflow')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/120.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are accessible to anonymous users at the time of writing this report."
              },
